\section{Avoidance Source Diagnosis}\label{sec:avoidance_source}

Fine-tuning on collision-free demonstrations produces genuine collision avoidance: LoRA-adapted Pi0.5 raises CAR by ${\sim}$26 percentage points with zero action-collapsed episodes. Does SDF geometric supervision contribute additional avoidance beyond what demonstrations provide? We compare LoRA-only against LoRA+SDF across a 20$\times$ range of loss weights ($\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$) on Pi0.5, all evaluated at the 30K-step checkpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Avoidance source attribution on SafeLIBERO. SDF shows no statistically significant CAR improvement beyond LoRA-only at the tested scale ($p > 0.05$ for $\lambda \in \{0.01, 0.1\}$). All fine-tuned models exhibit 0\% action-collapse rate and displacement $>$\,0.37\,m.}
\label{tab:avoidance_source}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Method & Seeds & TSR (\%) & CAR (\%) & EE Disp (m) \\
\midrule
Pi0.5 baseline & 1 & 71.5 & 28.5 & 0.367 \\
\midrule
LoRA-only (no SDF) & 3 & 49.7\,$\pm$\,3.2 & 54.83\,$\pm$\,2.25 & 0.391 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.005$ & 3 & 54.0\,$\pm$\,3.0 & 54.5\,$\pm$\,2.29 & 0.387 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.01$ & 3 & 53.8\,$\pm$\,1.5 & 56.33\,$\pm$\,1.61 & 0.389 \\
LoRA+SDF $\lambda{=}0.1$ & 3 & 50.2\,$\pm$\,3.9 & 57.00\,$\pm$\,2.18 & 0.394 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1pt}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:avoidance_source} shows that collision-free demonstrations alone raise CAR by ${\sim}$26 percentage points (28.5\% $\to$ 54.83\%) with zero action-collapsed episodes and displacement above 0.37\,m, confirming genuine avoidance. Adding SDF supervision does not yield statistically significant improvement at the tested scale: LoRA-only CAR (54.83\%) is statistically indistinguishable from all SDF conditions including $\lambda = 0.1$ (57.00\%, $p = 0.155$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_null}). A Phase~2 ablation ($\lambda = 0$ after step 5K) yields CAR\,=\,57.5\%\footnote{At the 18K checkpoint (13K steps of Phase~2 training, single seed s2); full 30K training did not complete due to disk constraints.}, suggesting that SDF gradients do not produce a lasting behavioral effect. The per-task CAR ordering is preserved across all conditions (chocolate\_pudding highest, bbq\_sauce lowest) and invariant to $\lambda$ across the tested range, indicating that avoidance patterns reflect the demonstration distribution rather than geometric supervision.

\paragraph{CAR--TSR trade-off.}\label{sec:tsr_collapse}
Fine-tuning degrades TSR by ${\sim}$18--22\,pp (71.5\% $\to$ 49.7--54.0\%), a consequence of distributional shift from the pre-trained prior rather than learned paralysis (all models retain $>$\,0.37\,m displacement and 0\% action-collapsed episodes). The SSR/TSR ratio increases from 0.39 (baseline) to 0.51--0.60 (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_ssr}): among task-completing episodes, a larger fraction avoids collisions after fine-tuning.

\paragraph{Dose-response at higher loss weights.}\label{sec:lambda_high}
Increasing $\lambda$ by 20$\times$ to 0.1 yields CAR\,=\,57.00\%\,$\pm$\,2.18 (3 seeds: 58.0\%, 58.5\%, 54.5\%), within the null distribution ($p = 0.155$\footnote{One-tailed $p = 0.071$; even with the directional prior that SDF should not \emph{decrease} CAR, the result remains non-significant.}; Figure~\ref{fig:lambda_car}). The binding constraint is not signal magnitude but the structural mismatch between EE monitoring and full-body collision surfaces.

\paragraph{Robustness to null construction.}
Pooling LoRA-only and $\lambda = 0.005$ seeds as null (Null~B, $\binom{9}{3} = 84$ permutations, $p = 0.155$) or using only LoRA-only seeds (Null~A, $\binom{6}{3} = 20$ permutations, $p = 0.250$) both yield non-significance at $\alpha = 0.05$.

\paragraph{Statistical power.}
With $n = 3$ seeds per group and $\sigma \approx 2.3$\,pp (LoRA cross-seed standard deviation), a two-sample $t$-test at $\alpha = 0.05$ (one-tailed) achieves 80\% power only for effects $\geq 5.6$\,pp. The observed effect of ${\sim}$2.2\,pp falls well below this minimum detectable effect (MDE), so the study is underpowered to detect a benefit of this magnitude.

\paragraph{Evaluation-seed consistency.}
Under matched evaluation conditions (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_eval_seed}), SDF $\lambda = 0.1$ yields higher CAR in all three paired eval seeds ($+$1.0, $+$2.5, $+$5.5\,pp), with lower cross-seed variance ($\sigma = 0.9$\,pp vs.\ 2.2\,pp for LoRA). The observed effect (${\sim}$2.2\,pp) falls below the MDE (${\sim}$5.6\,pp); whether this directional pattern reflects a genuine small benefit or residual confounding cannot be resolved at $n = 3$.

