\section{The Action-Collapsed Safety Problem}\label{sec:fake_safety}

Standard CAR assumes that collision-free episodes reflect deliberate avoidance. This assumption is systematically violated: in every Pi0 configuration tested, over 99\% of collision-free episodes are action-collapse artifacts (Table~\ref{tab:fake_safety}).\footnote{Pi0-family values report obstacle displacement from the original protocol; Pi0.5 reports EE displacement from the rotation-corrected protocol (\S\ref{sec:dfcar}). Sub-millimeter displacement indicates action collapse in both cases.} Applying dfCAR collapses all three to ${\approx}$\,0\%. Pi0+A1 achieves the \emph{highest} raw CAR (91.5\%) because more complete action collapse (from a parameter-name mismatch preventing LoRA loading; Table~\ref{tab:configurations}) yields greater CAR inflation. Pi0.5 validates the metric from the opposite direction: every safe episode exceeds 200\,mm displacement, and dfCAR equals raw CAR (28.5\%). The displacement distribution is sharply bimodal (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_displacement}), with a five-order-of-magnitude gap that makes $\tau_d$ robust.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Action-collapse decomposition on SafeLIBERO. dfCAR excludes episodes with displacement $< \tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. Pi0+A1 dfCAR$\,\approx\,$0: only 3/732 safe episodes exceed the threshold.}
\label{tab:fake_safety}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular*}{\linewidth}{l@{\extracolsep{\fill}}ccccc}
\toprule
Method & Ep. & CAR (\%) & TSR (\%) & Collapsed (\%) & dfCAR (\%) \\
\midrule
Pi0 Full-FT  & 200 & 83.0 & 0.5 & 99.4 & $\approx$\,0 \\
Pi0 LoRA     & 200 & 82.0 & 0.0 & ${\sim}$99 & $\approx$\,0 \\
Pi0+A1 (SDF) & 800 & 91.5 & 0.0 & 99.6 & $\approx$\,0 \\
\midrule
Pi0.5  & 200 & 28.5 & 71.5 & 0.0 & 28.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular*}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_tsr_car_scatter.pdf}
\caption{CAR inflation from action collapse. Solid dots: reported CAR; hollow dots: displacement-filtered dfCAR. The gap (red lines) quantifies illusory safety---Pi0-family models show 82--91\,pp inflation, while Pi0.5 and fine-tuned variants show no inflation (dfCAR\,=\,CAR).}
\label{fig:tsr_car_scatter}
\end{figure}
Figure~\ref{fig:tsr_car_scatter} quantifies CAR inflation from action collapse.\label{sec:anticorrelation} Pi0-family models show 82--91\,pp gaps between reported CAR and dfCAR: virtually all apparent safety is illusory. Pi0.5 and fine-tuned variants show no inflation (dfCAR\,=\,CAR), confirming genuine avoidance. Under dfCAR, the apparent safety--capability tradeoff disappears: high raw CAR was an artifact of action-collapse contamination, not deliberate avoidance.

\section{End-Effector-Only SDF Cannot Ensure Full-Body Safety}\label{sec:ee_only}

Could a perfect SDF signal enable effective EE-only refinement? We replace the learned SDF with ground-truth distance fields from the simulation geometry (\S\ref{sec:oracle_setup}). Table~\ref{tab:oracle} and Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_bar} show that all three oracle variants achieve \emph{lower} CAR than unrefined baselines: the best oracle (76.9\%) falls 5.1 points below LoRA (82.0\%). Refinement with perfect distance information produces more collisions, not fewer.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Oracle SDF refinement with ground-truth distance fields. All oracle variants achieve lower CAR than unrefined baselines. Per-task breakdown in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_pertask_oracle}.}
\label{tab:oracle}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & CAR (\%) & Collapsed (\%) & Refines/ep \\
\midrule
LoRA baseline (no refine) & 82.0 & 99.4 & -- \\
Full-FT baseline (no refine) & 83.0 & -- & -- \\
\midrule
Oracle-adaptive (GT, dyn.\ $\alpha$) & 75.0 & 98.3 & 14.2 \\
Oracle-fixed (GT, $\alpha{=}0.005$) & 73.1 & 98.3 & 14.8 \\
Oracle-feature (GT, feat.\ space) & 76.9 & 96.7 & 241.7 \\
\midrule
A3 learned SDF & 77.1 & -- & 0.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Per-task analysis reveals the mechanism (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_pertask_oracle}). On the one task where the EE is the primary collision body, oracle refinement helps (+9 points). On the remaining three, the SDF gradient pushes the EE away from obstacles but rotates intermediate arm links into obstacle volumes; the net effect is one collision traded for another. The A3 learned SDF never triggers refinement (0 steps per episode).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_oracle_bar.pdf}
\caption{CAR for oracle SDF refinement versus unrefined baselines. All GT oracle variants underperform baselines despite perfect distance information.}
\label{fig:oracle_bar}
\end{figure}
EE-only refinement is structurally counterproductive: joint-space coupling between EE displacement and arm-link rotation redistributes collisions to unmonitored links regardless of signal quality.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

Across 100+ experiments and 6000+ evaluation episodes on two VLA model families, three findings converge: CAR-based VLA safety evaluation on SafeLIBERO is structurally unreliable. Action collapse renders reported CAR illusory (\S\ref{sec:dfcar}); EE-only oracle refinement is counterproductive (\S\ref{sec:ee_only}); and collision-free demonstrations account for nearly all measured avoidance gains, with EE-only SDF contributing no statistically significant improvement (\S\ref{sec:avoidance_source}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_lambda_curve}
\caption{Effect of SDF loss weight $\lambda$ on CAR and TSR. $\lambda = 0$: LoRA-only. The $\lambda \in \{0.01, 0.1\}$ values remain within the null band ($p > 0.05$), consistent with the absence of statistically significant improvement across a 20$\times$ loss-weight range.}
\label{fig:lambda_car}
\end{figure}
\paragraph{Why EE-only SDF fails.}
Two independent causes explain the null result. \textit{(i)~Demonstration data subsumes the SDF signal.} Collision-free demonstrations implicitly encode spatial avoidance; the ${\sim}$26\,pp CAR gain arises from this distributional shift, with per-task ordering invariant across all SDF conditions (\S\ref{sec:avoidance_source}). Even at $\lambda = 0.1$, CAR remains within the null distribution ($p = 0.155$). We emphasize that our conclusion is absence of a detectable benefit at our experimental scale; a genuine 2--3\,pp effect remains consistent with our data but would require substantially larger experiments to resolve. \textit{(ii)~EE monitoring cannot prevent full-body collisions.} The SDF gradient acts on a single point while collisions occur along intermediate links; ground-truth oracle refinement \emph{reduces} CAR by 5--10\,pp (\S\ref{sec:ee_only}) because pushing the EE away rotates unmonitored links through obstacles. These causes are independent; effective geometric safety requires both full-body supervision and training signals beyond demonstrations.

\paragraph{Implications for evaluation.}
dfCAR corrects action-collapse contamination using only EE position logging, already available in most evaluation pipelines. We recommend reporting both CAR and dfCAR as a diagnostic complement; the gap quantifies contamination across a ${\sim}$4000$\times$ parameter range.

\paragraph{Future work.}
Three directions follow: (1) full-body SDF supervision along the kinematic chain; (2) cross-architecture evaluation on OpenVLA, RT-2, and other VLA families; (3) planning-layer safety constraints in joint space integrating with constrained-optimization frameworks.

\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

All experiments use MuJoCo simulation (SafeLIBERO, two VLA families, four pick-and-place tasks). Action collapse is structurally general but untested on other benchmarks and real hardware. The EE-only SDF null result is specific to auxiliary loss integration and should not be extrapolated to alternative pathways (Section~\ref{sec:related}). The sim-to-real gap introduces uncertainty: collision detection precision and contact dynamics differ on physical hardware. However, the core diagnostic---near-zero motor output inflating CAR---is kinematic; dfCAR requires only EE position logging and applies on physical platforms. Statistical power is limited by $n{=}3$ seeds per condition (MDE ${\approx}$ 5.6\,pp at 80\% power); a genuine SDF benefit below this threshold would go undetected, and larger-scale experiments could reveal effects in the 2--5\,pp range that our design cannot resolve. Oracle experiments used largely action-collapsed baselines; validation on actively moving policies and full-body SDF remain necessary before generalizing the counterproductive finding to non-collapsed settings. Run-to-run variance (same model, training seed, evaluation seed) is approximately 0.5\,pp CAR and 5.5\,pp TSR, bounding measurement precision.

